WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.310
- Welcome back to a special hour y'all.

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.230
We are unlocking our vault to showcase

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.140
amazing interview moments
you've never seen before.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.659
And today it's all about perfect pairs.

00:00:09.659 --> 00:00:12.659
(upbeat rock music)

00:00:16.909 --> 00:00:18.570
All right, all right.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.210
Um, I count actors Ed
Helms and Randall Park

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.530
among the best of them.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.677
They were here promoting their show,

00:00:23.677 --> 00:00:25.320
"True Story With Ed and Randall,"

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.720
and we decided to mix
things up and take advantage

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.670
of the sunny weather here
in Southern California

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.590
by bringing our conversation to the roof.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.270
One thing we quickly learned

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.460
is we all had in common,
it was classic TV,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.900
so just take a look.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.000
Uh, but Randall you've said
that you were raised by TV,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.890
which I feel like a
lot of us kind of were,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.990
I was raised by a single
mom for a lot of my life,

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.640
so I literally was in
front of a TV as well.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.340
Do you feel like, what
sitcoms kind of raised you?

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.680
- Oh my gosh. I was, I grew up on, on,

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.293
I knew every sitcom, like on the schedule

00:00:52.293 --> 00:00:56.820
and, and my entire like outlook
on real life was just like

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.850
based on the stories in these sitcoms.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:00.683
- Yeah.

00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:04.020
- Uh, there, uh, one of my
favorites was "Silver Spoons."

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:04.893
Remember "Silver Spoons"?

00:01:04.893 --> 00:01:05.726
- Yeah, of course.

00:01:05.726 --> 00:01:06.559
- Ricky Schroder.

00:01:06.559 --> 00:01:07.392
- [Kelly] No way. I don't know that one.

00:01:07.392 --> 00:01:08.225
- Oh, you're too young.

00:01:08.225 --> 00:01:09.058
- [Kelly] Okay dang.

00:01:09.058 --> 00:01:09.891
- Yeah, you're too young.

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:10.724
- [Kelly] Oh, I was like ...

00:01:10.724 --> 00:01:12.480
- Stream it. Is it streaming?

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.830
- This is what we're learning.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.307
- [Ed] That's all you could do now.

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:16.140
- [Randall] "Family Ties."

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.153
- "Family Ties." I love "Family Ties."

00:01:18.153 --> 00:01:19.740
- Great. Great. Yeah. All of 'em.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.560
- Mine was like "Cosby Show,"
like "Designing Women."

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.640
And "I Love Lucy."

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:24.810
Like "I Love Lucy" was my,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.490
that was like one of my favorite things

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:27.750
that my mom probably thought I was weird.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.300
All my friends were playing
and I was like, "no, it's on."

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.133
- It's the best.

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:31.966
- [Kelly] I was like, I loved her.

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:33.233
- "Dick Van Dyke Show." Another one.

00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:34.147
- "Chips"?

00:01:34.147 --> 00:01:35.790
- [Kelly] "Chips." I saw "Chips."

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:36.623
- I liked "Chips."

00:01:36.623 --> 00:01:37.456
- [Kelly] I don't know
that it raised me, but yes.

00:01:37.456 --> 00:01:38.575
- All right. All right. Yeah.

00:01:38.575 --> 00:01:39.492
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:39.492 --> 00:01:41.340
- I dunno. Um.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.480
- [Randall] You were, Ed
was raised by "Chips."

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.430
- I'm just trying, I'm
just trying to participate.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.260
- I shouldn't own this
and we might cut this out

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.330
but I did once get pulled over in LA,

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:52.380
this was like 15 years ago
and I wasn't doing anything.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.350
And this guy on a
motorcycle and it was like,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.320
even his horn was sad,
it was a sad cop sound,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.670
like it was like, it was like, beep beep,

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.950
and it was like a banana
seat bike, like horn.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:02.820
It was weird.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.440
And he pulled me over and he was like,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.350
he just acted like such a jerk.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:08.880
And I was like, okay "Chips,"

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:09.960
has it been a hard day?

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:10.793
And I ...

00:02:10.793 --> 00:02:12.359
- [Randall] You said "Chips"?

00:02:12.359 --> 00:02:13.192
(Ed and Kelly laugh)

00:02:13.192 --> 00:02:15.120
- Cause I was like, you
just totally pulled me over

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.130
for half my taillight being out like

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.300
and giving me a ticket?

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.770
Like, you didn't even gimme a warning?

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.416
- [Randall] Did he respond to "Chips"?

00:02:21.416 --> 00:02:24.583
- Pretty sure I got the ticket
because I called him "Chips."

00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:25.830
- He's like you're coming downtown.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.710
- Yeah. But he was so mean and
I was so lovely at the time

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.000
cause I was like, "oh, I'm
so sorry. What was I doing?"

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.760
And then just proceeded to
act like such a mean person.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:33.593
Anyway.

00:02:33.593 --> 00:02:34.426
- Yeah. Don't, don't call a cop "Chips."

00:02:34.426 --> 00:02:35.610
- [Kelly] I called him "Chips."

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:36.443
- [Ed] Yeah.

00:02:36.443 --> 00:02:37.276
- It was worth the ticket.

00:02:37.276 --> 00:02:38.340
(Ed, Kelly and Randall laugh)

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.683
I'll tell you what, I'd
pay it all over again.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:45.030
It was, he was not nice and
most cops are nice for me.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.800
Whatever. Not that I get
pulled over a lot. What?

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:47.658
Um.

00:02:47.658 --> 00:02:48.491
(Ed, Kelly and Randall laugh)

00:02:48.491 --> 00:02:49.324
In my twenties.

00:02:49.324 --> 00:02:50.940
Did y'all ever work in
the food service industry?

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.010
I was a waitress. I feel like
all of us have been waiters

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.723
or some form of food industry.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.560
I'm gonna ask you questions
right before you shoveled

00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.580
soup in your mouth,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.160
just to make it real fun and
exciting and challenging.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.423
I hope you have pepper in your teeth.

00:03:03.423 --> 00:03:05.160
- Should I talk with like tons of, of peas

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.515
like falling outta my mouth?

00:03:06.515 --> 00:03:08.070
(Kelly and Randall laugh)

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.875
- Um, I'll go first.

00:03:09.875 --> 00:03:13.170
- Well, I, I'll tell you about mine.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.258
No.

00:03:14.258 --> 00:03:15.150
(Kelly and Ed laugh)

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.717
- [Kelly] It was all I (indistinct)

00:03:16.717 --> 00:03:19.217
- That was. I can't tell
if that was funny or not.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.690
- [Randall] Probably disturbing, yeah.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:22.523
- It was awesome.

00:03:22.523 --> 00:03:24.690
- Um, I worked in a
concession stand at like

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.980
our little community, like, pool area.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.480
And um,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.250
it was a very,

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.100
like, not notable experience
other than the fact that

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.500
the whole point of the job for us

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.230
as like, cause I think I
was 13 or 14 when I started.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:41.063
- Oh wow.

00:03:41.063 --> 00:03:44.370
- We would put, we just would
sabotage the next person

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.470
in the sh, who's, whoever
had the next shift.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.950
So like, if I was closing
at night, I would put like

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.500
cups of nacho cheese on the ceiling fan,

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.570
so that when the person
came in the next morning

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:58.140
and then they'd like, be like
a tornado of nacho cheese

00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.280
that they'd have to clean up.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.431
And that's what we did do each other.

00:04:00.431 --> 00:04:02.840
- And probably bugs. Because it attracted?

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.509
- Oh. Oh, just a million
health code violations.

00:04:04.509 --> 00:04:05.789
(Kelly, Randall and crew laugh)

00:04:05.789 --> 00:04:08.760
Yeah. But, but it was
so fun to just like mess

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.140
with the each other.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.690
Cause you'd have like 45
minutes to open and you got,

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.910
it's like, "oh, okay, what
am I cleaning up today?"

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:15.930
- Oh my gosh.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:17.220
I hope they got you back.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:18.630
Um, my first job was a movie theater.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.970
So I worked concessions,
in like, box office.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.950
We used to do this thing where it's like,

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:24.480
I mean I'm telling myself, but

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.490
you could only eat the damaged candy,

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.650
if somebody brought back and
it was like a damaged box.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.260
So like sometimes we would just like

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.690
chunk a Hot Tamales box on the ground.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.270
And be like, "oh my gosh,
that is so damaged."

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.193
- What happened to this box?

00:04:37.193 --> 00:04:38.820
- And then we'd eat it. Yes.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.890
So, it's bad to work in that environment.

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.240
Even if it's just eating a bunch of stuff

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.290
you wouldn't have eaten

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:44.190
cause you're bored.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.023
- Yeah.

00:04:45.023 --> 00:04:46.357
- You guys are wild.

00:04:46.357 --> 00:04:47.190
(Kelly laughs)

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:48.666
So I worked at a Starbucks.

00:04:48.666 --> 00:04:49.499
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:04:49.499 --> 00:04:51.450
- And this is like not too long ago.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.640
Uh, and you know what we used to do,

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:54.949
we used to pee in the coffee.

00:04:54.949 --> 00:04:55.800
- [Kelly] What?

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.749
- I'm kidding. I'm kidding. I'm kidding.

00:04:57.749 --> 00:05:00.300
(Randall laughs)

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.133
I'm joking.

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:02.790
- Oh my god. Everyone's freaking out

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.039
in a drive-through of Starbucks right now.

00:05:05.039 --> 00:05:05.940
- We didn't, no, we didn't do that.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.861
I just tried to one up you
guys and it didn't work.

00:05:09.861 --> 00:05:10.694
Um.

00:05:10.694 --> 00:05:11.956
No, uh.

00:05:11.956 --> 00:05:14.046
- You're gonna to get sued anyway.

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:15.539
Even though, even
though, you were kidding.

00:05:15.539 --> 00:05:16.410
- Make it a great story. Yeah.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.790
- No, I worked at a Starbucks.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.740
I was, I was just started acting

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.780
so I needed a job that was flexible,

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:23.730
allowed me to audition, and Starbucks was,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.700
it was one of my favorite
jobs. And I remember,

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.650
I worked at the one, uh,

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:32.040
in the Palisades and you know,
fancy people lived there.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.320
And I remember, uh one day, again,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.500
I'm a struggling actor,
uh ,Brad Garrett came in

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:39.720
and I was like, "oh my
God, that's Brad Garrett."

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.660
And he was just as tall as you'd expect.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.940
And he had this booming voice and, uh,

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.139
and he left a, a $20 tip.

00:05:47.139 --> 00:05:47.972
- [Kelly] Oh that's nice.

00:05:47.972 --> 00:05:48.805
- Which was really sweet.

00:05:48.805 --> 00:05:50.130
- I promised myself that
whenever I was a waiter

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:51.310
that I would tip well.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:52.143
- [Randall] Yes.

00:05:52.143 --> 00:05:53.970
- I was like, anytime I go
somewhere, if I ever make money,

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.470
I'm gonna tip well because

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.230
sometimes you work your tail
off and yeah, you get nothing.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.250
- Oh yeah. Yeah, for sure.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.000
- I was like, I won't be that tool.

